**Summary rule:**
- Identify the cross: a vertical line and a horizontal line of the same non-zero color (color 3 in examples). The cross consists of all cells where either the row index equals the row of the horizontal line or the column index equals the column of the vertical line, and the cell color is that non-zero color.
- Determine the cross row and cross column: they are the row index where the horizontal line spans all columns (i.e., the row where all cells are that color), and the column index where the vertical line spans all rows (i.e., the column where all cells are that color). In both examples, the cross color is 3.
- For each cell in the grid:
  - If the cell is on the cross (i.e., grid[r][c] == cross_color and (r == cross_row or c == cross_col)), keep its color unchanged (should be cross_color).
  - Else, compute Chebyshev distance d = max(|r - cross_row|, |c - cross_col|).
  - If d is even, set the cell to color 4.
  - If d is odd, set the cell to 0 (background).
- Note: The input grid may have other non-zero cells? In examples, only the cross is non-zero. But the rule should handle that: only cells that are part of the cross (i.e., same color and on the line) are preserved; all others are overwritten based on parity of Chebyshev distance.

